# DESCRIPTION

linlon drives an Arm China Linlon-D6 display engine, which is Arm's Mali-D71 design, and serves the display class (`todhchai.display.device`'s `Display`, version 3) by flipping only.
The output firmware lit at boot keeps its mode, its link and its DisplayPort transmitter, and only the buffer its layer scans out changes.
devmgr(8) starts one for each engine, five on the CIX Sky1, and mounts its tree at `dev/display<n>`:

```text
driver linlon
class display
match acpi CIXH5010
dma-bits 40
```

An engine that isn't powered stops the machine on its first register read, so linlon first reads `/dev/acpi/power`, which says what the engine's power resources' `_STA` say until a driver writes it (devmgr(8)).
If they are off, firmware didn't light this engine. linlon then reads no register, and its display has no outputs.

Otherwise it probes the engine:
- `CORE_ID` must name a part with the D71's registers: a Linlon-D6 (product `0x0060`), Mali-D71 or Mali-D32;
- the global control unit must be in an active mode, which gives the pipeline that scans out;
- that pipeline's enabled layer gives the buffer, its stride and its format, which linlon saves;
- the timing controller gives the mode;
- the refresh is measured over 30 frames of the output unit's once-a-frame interrupt, which is then masked again.

The display's one output, 0, is the layer's size.
The engine has no AUX channel of its own: the EDID is its DisplayPort transmitter's, which dptx(3) reads.
devmgr(8) mounts the transmitter's tree at linlon's `/dev/link`, and a thread follows its `link` (display-link-link(5)): the monitor there and its EDID, at once and at each change.
The output is enabled while the link says a monitor is there; `edid` gives the EDID the link last gave, empty without one; and each change is told to every client as `outputsChanged`.
Without a link, the output is enabled and has no EDID.

The engine reads memory through the display SMMU: devmgr(8) gives linlon a BTI for the engine's named component, which translates all five of its stream ids, and the IORT's reserved range keeps firmware's framebuffer reachable at its own address.
`attach` pins a client's image through that BTI as one run of device addresses, which the layer reads as it is: nothing is copied.
Where no IOMMU translates the engine, an image not contiguous in physical memory can't be pinned so, and `attach` fails, `unavailable`.

`present` shows an image the output's size: it writes the damaged rows back from the CPU's caches, since the Sky1's engines don't snoop them, points the layer at the image as XRGB8888, sets the pipeline's configuration-valid bit, and waits for the configuration to latch at the next frame: the GCU's interrupt, or its raw status after 100 ms.
So `present` returns once its frame is the one being scanned out, and a client that draws into an image while another is shown, as radharc(4) does, never tears.
`show` of an image is `present` of the whole image.
`flush` of the shown image writes its damage back and waits for the latch the same way; it tears where the client draws into the image being scanned out.
A flush of an image that isn't shown does nothing.
`show` of image 0 puts back firmware's buffer, its stride and its format; `detach` of the shown image does so first, so the engine never reads pages unpinned.

# FILES

The tree is served in devmgr(8)'s, at `drivers/linlon-acpi-_SB_.DPU<n>`, and mounted at `dev/display<n>`.

: `display`
  The display class's service.
: `status`
  Read only.
  Either `off: ...`, where the engine is unpowered and was left alone, or the probe's lines: `powered by firmware`; `Linlon-D6 r<n>p<n>, ARCH_ID <hex>, <n> pipelines, <n> blocks`; `GCU: output 0 active (status <hex>)`; `pipeline <n>, layer <n>: <w>x<h> <format>, stride <n>, at <address>`; `timing <w>x<h>, total <w>x<h> (h <front>/<sync>/<back>, v ...)`; `refresh <hz> Hz over <n> frames`; and `ready: output 0 <w>x<h>`.
  Where the probe stopped, its last line is `idle: firmware left it not scanning out` or `FAILED: <why>`.
  Once ready, two more lines: `images <n>, refused <n>, showing firmware's buffer` (or `image <id>`), where `refused` counts images `attach` couldn't pin as one run, and `flips <n>, latched <n>, missed interrupts <n>, timeouts <n>`, where a missed interrupt is a latch seen in the raw status at the deadline.
  Following a link, one more: `link: connected, <maker> <name>, preferred <w>x<h> at <hz> Hz, changes <n>` (or `no edid`), or `link: no monitor, changes <n>`.

